INFO 03-13 05:29:39 logger.py:39] Received request chatcmpl-434b04fea6974306b106a9ddbc504424: prompt: "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>Shorten the current theorem (wrapped in <CURRENT>...</CURRENT>) to be as short as possible in length - measured in the number of tactics in the proof - while also ensuring that the output is still a correct proof of the theorem. The proof context, with relevant definitions and theorems, has additionally been provided to help you better understand the proof and ensure the correctness of your response. It is wrapped in <CONTEXT>...</CONTEXT>, with each item wrapped in <ITEM>...</ITEM>. Include the output in the <IMPROVED>...</IMPROVED> tag.\n\n<CONTEXT>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Subtype.val_injective\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[simp] theorem val_injective : Injective (@val _ p) :=\n  coe_injective\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=div_eq_one\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive]\ntheorem div_eq_one : a / b = 1 ↔ a = b :=\n  ⟨eq_of_div_eq_one, fun h ↦ by rw [h, div_self']⟩\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=add_right_cancel_iff\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=div_mul_cancel\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive (attr := simp)]\ntheorem div_mul_cancel (a b : G) : a / b * b = a := by\n  rw [div_eq_mul_inv, inv_mul_cancel_right a b]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=one_div_one_div\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive]\ntheorem one_div_one_div : 1 / (1 / a) = a := by simp\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=mul_le_mul_iff_left\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive (attr := simp)]\ntheorem mul_le_mul_iff_left [MulLeftMono α]\n    [MulLeftReflectLE α] (a : α) {b c : α} :\n    a * b ≤ a * c ↔ b ≤ c :=\n  rel_iff_cov α α (· * ·) (· ≤ ·) a\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=rfl\n--context_item_type=def\n/--\n`rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the\nsame as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.\n\nThis is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although\nthe statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is\ndefinitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in\nLean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality.\n-/\n@[match_pattern] def rfl {α : Sort u} {a : α} : Eq a a := Eq.refl a\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=LT.lt.false\n--context_item_type=theorem\nprotected theorem false [Preorder α] {x : α} : x < x → False :=\n  lt_irrefl x\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=add_left_cancel_iff\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=mul_one_div\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive]\ntheorem mul_one_div (x y : G) : x * (1 / y) = x / y := by\n  rw [div_eq_mul_inv, one_mul, div_eq_mul_inv]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=symm\n--context_item_type=theorem\nlemma symm [IsSymm α r] : a ≺ b → b ≺ a := IsSymm.symm _ _\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Classical.choose_spec\n--context_item_type=theorem\ntheorem choose_spec {α : Sort u} {p : α → Prop} (h : ∃ x, p x) : p (choose h) :=\n  (indefiniteDescription p h).property\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=add_comm\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Classical.choose\n--context_item_type=def\n/--\nGiven that there exists an element satisfying `p`, returns one such element.\n\nThis is a straightforward consequence of, and equivalent to, `Classical.choice`.\n\nSee also `choose_spec`, which asserts that the returned value has property `p`.\n-/\nnoncomputable def choose {α : Sort u} {p : α → Prop} (h : ∃ x, p x) : α :=\n  (indefiniteDescription p h).val\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=div_le_iff₀\n--context_item_type=theorem\n/-- See `div_le_iff₀'` for a version with multiplication on the other side. -/\nlemma div_le_iff₀ (hc : 0 < c) : b / c ≤ a ↔ b ≤ a * c := by\n  rw [div_eq_mul_inv, mul_inv_le_iff₀ hc]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=mul_pos\n--context_item_type=theorem\nalias mul_pos := Left.mul_pos\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Subtype.mk_eq_mk\n--context_item_type=theorem\ntheorem mk_eq_mk {a h a' h'} : @mk α p a h = @mk α p a' h' ↔ a = a' :=\n  Subtype.ext_iff\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Positive.coe_inv\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[simp]\ntheorem coe_inv (x : { x : K // 0 < x }) : ↑x⁻¹ = (x⁻¹ : K) :=\n  rfl\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Positive.val_mul\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[simp]\ntheorem val_mul (x y : { x : R // 0 < x }) : ↑(x * y) = (x * y : R) :=\n  rfl\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Positive.coe_add\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[simp, norm_cast]\ntheorem coe_add (x y : { x : M // 0 < x }) : ↑(x + y) = (x + y : M) :=\n  rfl\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Subtype.ext_iff\n--context_item_type=theorem\n\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Eq.le\n--context_item_type=theorem\nalias Eq.le := le_of_eq\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=mul_inv_cancel\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive (attr := simp)]\ntheorem mul_inv_cancel (a : G) : a * a⁻¹ = 1 := by\n  rw [← inv_mul_cancel a⁻¹, inv_eq_of_mul (inv_mul_cancel a)]\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=one_div\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[to_additive (attr := simp)]\ntheorem one_div (a : G) : 1 / a = a⁻¹ :=\n  (inv_eq_one_div a).symm\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=Set.mem_singleton_iff\n--context_item_type=theorem\n@[simp]\ntheorem mem_singleton_iff {a b : α} : a ∈ ({b} : Set α) ↔ a = b :=\n  Iff.rfl\n</ITEM>\n<ITEM>\n--name=two_pos\n--context_item_type=theorem\nalias two_pos := zero_lt_two\n</ITEM>\n</CONTEXT>\n\n<CURRENT>\nproblem imo2022_p2 (f : ℝ+ → ℝ+) :\n    f ∈ solution_set ↔\n    ∀ x, ∃! y, x * f y + y * f x ≤ ⟨2, two_pos⟩ := by\n  constructor\n  · intro hf\n    simp only [Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hf\n    rw [hf] at *; clear hf\n    intro x\n    use x\n    constructor\n    · suffices h : (1:ℝ+) + 1 = ⟨2, two_pos⟩ by\n        simp only [one_div, mul_inv_cancel]\n        exact Eq.le h\n      norm_num [Subtype.ext_iff]\n    · intro y hxy\n      change (x * (1 / y) + y * (1 / x)).val ≤ _  at hxy\n      obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := x\n      obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := y\n      simp only [Positive.coe_add, Positive.val_mul, one_div, Positive.coe_inv] at hxy\n     